382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (By Rabbi Aron Moss)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (Early on October 7, 2023, as Israel prepared to celebrate Simchat Torah, Hamas terrorists breached the Gaza border by air, land, and sea under cover of heavy rocket fire. The assault killed approximately 1,200 people, mostly civilians, and took roughly 240 hostages. Its brutality, including extreme sexual violence and mutilation, shocked the world. War followed with Hamas and allied terrorist entities, alongside a surge of global antisemitism, leaving many of us asking: what can I do to help?)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (The Lubavitcher Rebbe taught that every Jew can respond by increasing in Torah observance and mitzvot. A mitzvah is never merely private: it connects us to G-d and to every Jew, everywhere. When one Jew performs a mitzvah in another's merit, the distance between them shrinks.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (Tefillin connect mind and heart to G-d; the Rebbe urged Jewish men and boys over thirteen to put them on, especially in times of danger. Shabbat candles, lit each Friday before sunset, symbolize peace and light overcoming darkness. Have your mezuzot checked by a qualified sofer.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (Prayer is something everyone can offer, even a few minutes daily for the safety of the Jewish people. Charity turns concern into direct action. Torah study, too, is a source of spiritual strength.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (Finally, there is unity itself. October 7 reminded Jews everywhere that we are one people, regardless of how observant we are. Reaching out to a Jewish friend, or inviting someone for Shabbat, strengthens that identity.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (The Torah describes the Jewish people rising like a lion, an image Rashi ties to the eagerness with which Jews greet each morning to perform mitzvot. That eagerness is a real response to uncertainty and pain. None of this replaces the effort to protect human life; it is a spiritual response, a way of standing with our brothers and sisters in Israel, even from a great distance.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [26]
>>
endobj
